# rustdoc

Sources: [rustdoc book](https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/rustdoc/how-to-write-documentation.html), API Guidelines C-EXAMPLE, C-FAILURE, C-LINK.

- English. `//!` only for crate and module docs. `///` on items. Line comments, not `/** */`.
- Do not copy Japanese `///` text from `docs/design.ja.md` into the implementation. Restate the behavior in English.
- First line: one punctuated sentence, third-person singular ("Returns…", not "Return…").
- Then why or when, then sections. Common headings, with plural `Examples` even for one: `# Examples`, `# Errors`, `# Panics`, `# Safety`.
- `# Errors` on every public `Result`. `# Panics` only if it can panic. Library paths should not panic (`.cursor/rules/rust.mdc`). `# Safety` is required if a later design change allows `unsafe`.
- Every item an external crate can name is documented and has a usage example: an `# Examples` code block, or a link to one on another public item whose example calls this item. A method may rely on the `# Examples` of its own type when that example calls it: docs.rs shows the method on the type's page, under that example. A method whose behavior is more than returning a value (a build, `planes`, `volume`, `boundary_cycle`) has its own block. Do not drop the example because the item looks obvious. `missing_docs` passing is not a reading of the text (`.cursor/rules/reader-docs.mdc`).
- Doc tests compile. Use `?`, not `unwrap`. Hide setup with `# ` lines. Fence with ` ```rust `.
- Link types with intra-doc links. Do not restate the signature.
- A link resolves in `cargo doc --no-deps --document-private-items`. When a function becomes a test-only wrapper (`#[cfg(test)]`) of a `_with` form, move every link to it onto the item that always exists. A public item does not link to a private one; name the value instead (`u32::MAX`).
- Public rustdoc (`//!` and `///` on an item an external crate can name) needs no Issue number, pull request number, phase ID, roadmap ID, design section (`design §9`), ADR number, `roadmap.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.cursor/rules`, Grill, developer `just` recipe, or private item's name: it states the behavior a caller relies on (`.cursor/rules/reader-docs.mdc`, Stranger context). Those pointers belong in `//` comments and in the rustdoc of private items, where the next reader of the code needs them.
- A number or a bound in the rustdoc of a private item names its source (Issue or PR) and the inputs it was measured on; in public rustdoc it names the inputs and the conditions, and the Issue goes in a `//` comment beside it. A claim covers only what the code or the measurement shows: "no arena insert is concurrent", not "no allocation is concurrent"; "under 1% on the sets of #26", not "at most 0.7%".
- Do not document "this field is ignored when…". If that sentence seems needed, the types are wrong (`.cursor/rules/types.mdc`).
- When `Cargo.toml` exists, `missing_docs` is deny on the library crate. `pub(crate)` items do not need rustdoc.
- A new item goes before the doc comment of the item that follows it, or after that item's end. Inserted between a doc comment and its item, it takes that doc: in #319 a new test took the doc of `the_bound_keeps_its_measured_margin`.
- An edit that inserts before an item anchors at the first line of that item's doc comment, not at its attribute or signature. After the edit, read the diff for a `///` block directly above the new item's own `///` block. #342 repeated the split of #319 twice, after #328 had made the rule above.
- Reading the diff did not catch it a third time: #490 split two doc comments, in `simplicial.rs` and `records.rs`. Before the PR, scan the diff mechanically for an added line directly below a context line that is a `///` line (`git diff -U1 main -- src`, one hunk at a time), and look at each hit. Run it as a script over the branch's whole diff against its base, every time before the pull request opens and again after another branch is merged in; a scan that depends on remembering is skipped. #549 skipped it and split two more, in `store.rs` and `records.rs`, which the review found.
- A change to what a module covers (a size, a stage, a range) updates every doc that states it: the module's own, its parent module's, and the item docs that repeat it. Search for the old statement (`k ≤ 4`) before the PR. #354 widened the first stage to k ≤ 5 and left "k ≤ 4" in the module docs of `predicates` and on `semi_static::sign`.
- Tests do not require a fixed Preconditions / Steps / Expected comment. The test's name and body should show which design invariant failed.

```rust
/// Returns the sign of the orientation of `points`.
///
/// The sign is the exact sign of the predicate in the design, not a rounded `f64`.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns an error when exact evaluation cannot obtain a work buffer.
```
